**Screen: solar, Atacama Region, Chile (-27.02535, -69.89853). Footprint: 1 km radius circle. Stage unknown, so I assumed greenfield.**

**Flags, most serious first**

1. **Traffic, worker influx and construction on the main road (PS4, medium).** Ruta C-17, a primary road, lies on the footprint, so construction traffic would use an existing route through the site. No school or health facility is mapped within 5 km. The nearest mapped settlement is an isolated dwelling about 8 km away (Las Posadas). The road is the access route, so this is a traffic and community-safety item, not a new-road item.

2. **Desert habitat and PV effects on biodiversity (PS6, medium).** No protected area, Ramsar site or wetland is mapped within 20 km, and no river or forest is mapped within 5 km. OSM coverage is patchy, though, so that is not proof of absence. The desert is natural habitat that is not farmed. Desk research found that solar plants in the Atacama change microclimate and arthropod composition beneath fixed-mount panels (Microclimatic conditions between panel mounts was found to be more extreme than in the surrounding desert; Springer, Environmental Management, 2017, https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00267-017-0906-4). Wider regional work notes that Copiapoa taxa... are threatened by metal mining operations (ResearchGate, Copiapoa extinction risk, https://www.researchgate.net/publication/383874811; undated in metadata). GBIF returned no CR or EN records within 5 km and one record within 20 km, with the species not named. That is a gap, not an all-clear.

3. **Land take with possibly unmapped customary or grazing use (PS5, low).** Arid land with nothing mapped on it commonly has unrecorded pastoral or artisanal use. An existing power plant and substation sit on the footprint, and the site is in an energy and mining cluster. That may mean the land is already an industrial estate, or that the mapped plant is the planned project itself. Land tenure has not been checked. The industrial cluster lowers severity, but the land status is still unknown.

4. **Colla and Diaguita peoples in the region (PS7, low; regional knowledge and desk research, not mapped data).** No Indigenous or community land is mapped within 20 km. Desk research says the Collas currently occupy part of the Atacama desert, some quebradas of the Andean foothills and the edge of the puna in the provinces of Copiapó and Chañaral (Marca Chile, Conadi source, https://www.marcachile.cl/en/los-10-principales-pueblos-indigenas-de-chile/; dated 15 days before search). The regional statistics show that 49.5% of the indigenous population in the region identify as Diaguita and 25.3% as Colla (Intendencia Regional de Atacama, 2019, https://goreatacama.gob.cl/wp-content/uploads/2019_06_03_I._ATACAMA.pdf). I did not place the site in a specific valley, and altitude is unknown, so this stays at low confidence.

